-- M4 (spec §10): the DOCUMENT-ASSEMBLY demo — a verdict screen that also
-- assembles the letter.
--
-- This is the capability that justifies the backend outside the room: the
-- citizen answers a handful of questions and leaves with a document, not just
-- a verdict. The subject is deliberately the same domain as `seam.l4` (a
-- landlord's notice to quit) because a landlord's notice is precisely the
-- document a verdict screen should hand back — but it is a SEPARATE file, so
-- that adding an attachment cannot silently rewrite `seam.l4`'s committed
-- byte goldens and make the demo indistinguishable from a regression.
--
-- THREE FIELDS THE RULE DOES NOT READ. The landlord's name, the tenant's name
-- and the property address decide nothing; they exist because the LETTER needs
-- them. That is not a wart, it is the point: an attachment extends the
-- interview's question set beyond what the rule alone would ask, and the
-- emitted interview has to seek them.
--
-- THE HAZARD, and why `variable name:` is mandatory. A failed attachment
-- evaluation inside a question block is caught by
-- `except BaseException as err: logmessage("Problem with attachments block: "…)
-- ; continue` (parse.py:9521-9526 at 1b6678384) — a log line, not an error.
-- Probing could not make a realistic defect take that path (a raising body
-- propagates ZeroDivisionError; an unaskable reference propagates
-- DAErrorMissingVariable, on both the question-attached and standalone paths),
-- so the loud failures are louder than the spec feared. The failure that is
-- genuinely SILENT is different and worse: a SUCCESSFUL EMPTY RENDER. The
-- interview completes, the variable is a healthy DAFileCollection, and the
-- letter is blank. Nothing raises and nothing is logged.
--
-- The only defence is a post-condition on the assembled content, and the only
-- way to have one is `variable name:` on the attachment — without it
-- docassemble stores the document under `_internal['docvar'][n]`
-- (parse.py:4997-5003) and there is nothing to assert about.

DECLARE Notice
    HAS
      @desc The name of the landlord serving the notice
      `the landlord name`        IS A STRING
      @desc The name of the tenant on whom the notice is served
      `the tenant name`          IS A STRING
      @desc The address of the let property
      `the property address`     IS A STRING
      @desc Whether the letting is of a dwelling occupied as a home
      `is a residential tenancy` IS A BOOLEAN
      @desc Whether the notice to quit was served in writing
      `written notice was given` IS A BOOLEAN
      @desc Length of the notice period served, in whole months
      `months of notice given`   IS A NUMBER

@export Whether the notice to quit is valid
GIVEN n IS A Notice @desc The notice to quit under assessment
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the notice to quit is valid` n IF
        n's `is a residential tenancy`
    AND n's `written notice was given`
    AND n's `months of notice given` AT LEAST 2

-- A valid notice: residential, in writing, three months.
#EVAL `the notice to quit is valid` OF
        Notice WITH
            `the landlord name`        IS "Marbury Estates Ltd"
            `the tenant name`          IS "A. Tenant"
            `the property address`     IS "14 Chancery Lane, Flat B"
            `is a residential tenancy` IS TRUE
            `written notice was given` IS TRUE
            `months of notice given`   IS 3
-- TRUE

-- Short notice: one month where two are required. The letter is still
-- assembled — a citizen who is told "no" is exactly the citizen who needs the
-- document explaining why.
#EVAL `the notice to quit is valid` OF
        Notice WITH
            `the landlord name`        IS "Marbury Estates Ltd"
            `the tenant name`          IS "A. Tenant"
            `the property address`     IS "14 Chancery Lane, Flat B"
            `is a residential tenancy` IS TRUE
            `written notice was given` IS TRUE
            `months of notice given`   IS 1
-- FALSE
